  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nNorthern Michigan remains squarely centered between Intermountain \nWest/High Plains upper level ridge/heat dome and northeast NOAM \nupper level troughing. Result is persistent northwest flow aloft, \nwith periodic embedded shortwave troughs/upper jet speed maxes \ncrossing the region. And, right on cue, next wave set to drop across \nour area later tonight into Monday, perhaps helping drive some \nconvective development as attendant forcing interacts with a \nmarginally favorable thermodynamic environment.\n\nAbove pattern looks to remain remarkably persistent heading through \nthis week into next weekend, keeping our airmasses of Canadian \norigin and locking all real heat well to our west. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nAddressing convective evolution and potential for severe \nthunderstorms later tonight and Monday. Temperature trends and \nadditional late week shower chances take secondary focus.\n\nDetails: \n\nJust your typical northern Michigan challenging convective forecast \ntonight and Monday. Confidence on how everything unfolds remains \nremarkably low. Definitely some concerns on just how extensive and \nstrong any showers/storms will be tonight and Monday, with at least \nsome hints best upper jet forcing remains northeast and detached \nfrom best thermodynamic environment across the western Great Lakes. \nDefinitely could see a scenario were deep convection developing well \nto our northwest this afternoon rides surface based instability \ngradient southeast across eastern Wisconsin into southern Lake \nMichigan later this evening into the overnight...with perhaps some \nadditional less organized showers/storms firing off to our northeast \nwhere upper jet forcing is maximized. Pattern recognition/conceptual \nmodels definitely support this idea as well...suggesting any \nshower/storm activity across our area tonight remains both less \norganized and intense than earlier anticipated. Simple best course \nof action is to continue to monitor radar trends heading through \nthis evening. Area remains in marginal to slight risk wording for \nsevere weather from SPC...although they themselves mention \nconsiderable uncertainty in placement of best juxtaposition of \nenhanced convective utilizing shear and deepest instability. If \nthose features can become better juxtaposed across our area then \ndefinitely could see some gusty wind potential with any more \norganized convective clusters tonight.\n\nAnd (unfortunately so) what transpires tonight will likely have \nplenty to say on how Monday unfolds. Uncertainty is way too high to \noffer any real specificity. With that said, could definitely see a \nscenario were a south diving convective complex to our west tonight \nsignificantly delays or even prevents any real organized convective \ndevelopment across our area Monday...with primary convective \ndevelopment along congealed cold pool forcing/differential heating \nboundaries to our west and southwest (this despite what appears to \nbe better overhead deep layer forcing from approaching shortwave and \nfavorable placement of upper jet forcing). Forcing should \ntheoretically be enough to drive at least some convection across our \narea, although again exact organization will likely be significantly \ntied to how things unfold tonight. Definitely something to monitor \nas better deep forcing does support some severe potential...as \nevident by SPC slight risk wording across all of our forecast area.\n\nForcing steadily departs Monday night, with large area of slow \nmoving surface high pressure expected to bring dry and near \nseasonable temperatures Tuesday through Thursday (although could see \nsome rather chilly overnight lows). Will need to watch for \nadditional southeast diving energy to end the week on into the \nweekend to drive additional shower/storm chances. Current trends \nsupport typical end of July/early August high temperatures in the \n70s and 80s to end the week into the weekend.",
